17] [17592186354174 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186352678 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354174 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186353139 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354174 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186353406 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354174 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354174 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354174 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186353139 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354174 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "d31711c3-cb15-50d5-862f-d3265b1300b3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354639 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354174 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354639 #edb/ref 1069 "q-88301" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354639 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354639 #edb/ref 1071 "Find\n$|\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 91 & 92 & 93 \\\\ 90 & 92 & 93\\end{aligned}|.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354639 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "26f8d807-4854-5fae-88e0-bb503698def0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354639 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that the $2$nd row of our determinant is almost equal to the $3$rd row. So, we can rewrite the given determinant, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 91 & 92 & 93 \\\\ 90 & 92 & 93\\end{aligned}| & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 90+1 & 92+0 & 93+0 \\\\ 90 & 92 & 93\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, using the linearity property of determinants, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 90+1 & 92+0 & 93+0 \\\\ 90 & 92 & 93\\end{aligned}| & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 90 & 92 & 93 \\\\ 90 & 92 & 93\\end{aligned}|+|\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 90 & 92 & 93\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first determinant must be zero since it has two identical rows, while the second determinant can be expanded across the $2$nd row, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 90 & 92 & 93 \\\\ 90 & 92 & 93\\end{aligned}|+|\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 90 & 92 & 93\\end{aligned}| & =0+1(-1)^{2+1}|\\begin{aligned}3 & 6 \\\\ 92 & 93\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-(3\\cdot 93-6\\cdot 92) \\\\  & =-(279-552) \\\\  & =273\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186428341 #edb/ref 1030 1.0 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186428341 #edb/ref 1032 "Initial Codex estimate from captured target-band crossing frequency. Selected account linear: 1 rises / 1 eligible passed source activities = 1.000000; nearest selected fraction = 1. Crossing activity numbers [1]; waits [1]. Separate account estimates: linear=1 (1/1). Selection: most observations, then lower weight, then account name; no averaging. Single-activity estimates included. Intervening target updates: False. Crossing frequency is the chosen practical estimate of encompassing coverage; a multi-band rise counts once. This does not interpret color steps as physical repetition units. Method: encompassing-report.md. Evidence: reference/mathacademy/encompassing-analysis-2026-10-11/frequency-fractions/linear-frequency-estimates.json, pair 1774->148. All account evidence retained in the prepared import's selected-relationships.json." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186428341 #edb/ref 1036 #edb/ref 17592186050765 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186435742 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186435771 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435742 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186435772 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435742 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186435773 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435742 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186435774 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435742 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186435775 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435742 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435742 #edb/ref 1045 "Further Properties of Determinants" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435742 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186435774 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435742 #edb/ref 1048 #edb/ref 1759218604665